<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nagarjuna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 150–250</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Nāgārjuna, Lóngshù</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nāgārjuna was a philosopher and Mahāyāna Buddhist monk from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a>, considered the founder of the Madhyamaka (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-way">Middle Way</a>) school. Very little is reliably known of his life: most scholars place him in South India between the 1st and 3rd centuries CE, and an association with the Sātavāhana dynasty conventionally dates him to around 150–250 CE. His Mūlamadhyamakakārikā (Root Verses on Madhyamaka) argues that all things are empty of svabhāva, or inherent existence, joining emptiness to dependent origination. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> calls him the &quot;second Buddha&quot;, and his writings shaped Indian, East Asian and Tibetan Buddhist thought.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nagarjuna.jpg" alt="Nagarjuna" width="330" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown | Recovered from Himalayan Art Resources · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nagarjuna_with_84_mahasiddha_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Nagarjuna</b>, in the 2nd or 3rd century CE, founded the Madhyamaka, or “middle way”, school with his analysis of emptiness; the brothers Asanga and Vasubandhu, in the 4th or 5th century, shaped the Yogacara school, which explored the workings of consciousness.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>…astronomy and mathematics in Sanskrit verse, Brahmagupta setting out rules for calculating with zero and negative numbers; the medical treatises of Charaka and Sushruta founded Ayurveda; and the schools of Hindu, Buddhist and Jain philosophy, from <b>Nagarjuna</b> to Shankara, debated in Sanskrit for more than a thousand years.</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
